'''''Third World Quarterly''''' (TWQ) is a [[peer-reviewed]] [[academic journal]] managed by Global South Ltd and published by [[Taylor & Francis]].<ref name="Taylor & Francis Online" /> The journal was founded in 1978 by the Pakistani journalist and former civil servant [[Altaf Gauhar]].<ref name="Simmons" /><ref name="TWQhistory" /> Its founding editor and chair of its editorial board is Shahid Qadir,<ref name="Taylor & Francis Online" /> who is also one of two directors of Global South Ltd.<ref>{{Cite web |title=GLOBAL SOUTH LTD overview – Find and update company information – GOV.UK |url=https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company/10959126 |access-date=2023-04-08 |website=find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Global South Ltd|website=Endole Insight |url=https://suite.endole.co.uk/insight/company/10959126-global-south-ltd}}</ref>

''Third World Quarterly'' is a leading journal in the field of [[Global South]] studies, focusing on the "historical and contemporary structures and relations of inequality and development" and covering [[international relations]], [[development studies]], [[gender studies]], [[race studies]], [[heterodox economics]], and [[historical]] and [[Sociocultural anthropology|sociocultural research]].<ref name=":0">{{Cite web |title=Learn about Third World Quarterly |url=https://www.tandfonline.com/journals/ctwq20/about-this-journal |access-date=2026-09-05 |website=Taylor & Francis |language=en}}</ref>

Although the journal's title suggests four issues per year, it is in fact published monthly.<ref name="Taylor & Francis Online" /> The journal had an [[impact factor]] of 2.4 in 2025.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Third World Quarterly {{!}} Journal metrics|url=https://www.tandfonline.com/action/journalInformation?show=journalMetrics&journalCode=ctwq20 |access-date=2026-08-29 |website=Taylor & Francis |language=en}}</ref>

==History==
The journal was founded by the Pakistani journalist and former civil servant [[Altaf Gauhar]]. According to his obituary in ''[[The Guardian]]'', Gauhar came to London in the mid-1970s and, with financial support from the [[Bank of Credit and Commerce International]], launched a number of initiatives intended to draw attention to the problems facing developing countries; these included ''Third World Quarterly'', the monthly magazine ''South'', and the Third World Foundation.<ref name="Simmons">{{Cite news |last=Simmons |first=Michael |title=Altaf Gauhar |work=The Guardian |date=13 December 2000 |url=https://www.theguardian.com/news/2000/dec/13/guardianobituaries.michaelsimmons |access-date=29 August 2026}}</ref>

Other early founders included [[Commonwealth Secretary-General]] [[Shridath Ramphal]] and [[Minister of Finance (Pakistan)|Pakistani Finance Minister]] [[Mahbub ul Haq|Mahbub ul-Haq]]. The publication has a tradition of featuring progressive and exploratory content that is anti-racist, anti-imperialist, and anti-sexist.<ref name=":0" /> [[Edward Said]] was a member of the publication's founding editorial board.<ref name="TWQhistory" />

The journal was launched in December 1978.<ref name="TWQhistory">{{Cite web |title=Third World Quarterly (TWQ) History |url=https://globalsouth.org/third-world-quarterly-twq-history/ |website=Global Souths Hub |access-date=29 August 2026}}</ref> Shahid Qadir joined as associate editor and became editor-in-chief in 1990. He held the title of editor from 1990 to 2020 and has been described as founding editor since 2020.<ref name="TWQhistory" /> Originally published quarterly, the journal moved to twelve issues per year in 2015.<ref name="TWQhistory" />

In September 2017, the journal published an article entitled "The Case for [[Colonialism]]" by political scientist [[Bruce Gilley]]. This article was described by Portia Roelofs and Max Gallien of the [[London School of Economics]] as "a travesty, the academic equivalent of a Trump tweet, clickbait with footnotes."<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.insidehighered.com/news/2017/09/26/author-third-world-quarterly-article-colonialism-wants-it-stricken-record-it-might|title=Author of 'Third World Quarterly' article on colonialism wants it stricken from the record, but it might not be going anywhere|first=Colleen|last=Flaherty|website=Inside Higher Education|date=2017-09-26|accessdate=2019-05-14}}</ref> [[University of Oxford|Oxford]] theologian [[Nigel Biggar]] himself became the [[Nigel Biggar#Colonialism|subject of controversy]] after defending Gilley's article. A large number of the journal's [[editorial board]] resigned in protest, citing a flawed peer review process for the colonialism submission and inaccurate statements from the editor-in-chief, Shahid Qadir.<ref>{{Cite news|author=Thomas, Rosamma|url=http://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/uk/journals-editorial-board-resigns-over-colonialism-essay/articleshow/60754617.cms|title=Journal's editorial board resigns over colonialism essay |work=The Times of India|date=19 September 2017|access-date=19 September 2017}}</ref> 15 out of the 34 members of the editorial board were signatories to the resignation letter, and a petition to retract the piece at [[Change.org]] had more than 10,000 supporters.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.insidehighered.com/news/2017/09/19/controversy-over-paper-favor-colonialism-sparks-calls-retraction|title=Controversy over a paper in favor of colonialism sparks calls for retraction|last=Flaherty|first=Colleen|date=19 September 2017|work=[[Inside Higher Education]]|access-date=19 September 2017}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news|url=http://www.chronicle.com/blogs/ticker/editorial-board-of-third-world-quarterly-resigns/120188|title=Editorial Board of 'Third World Quarterly' Resigns|last=Zamudio-Suaréz|first=Fernanda|date=20 September 2017|work=The Chronicle of Higher Education|access-date=23 September 2017}}</ref>

Board member [[Noam Chomsky]] opposed the retraction, saying: "Rebuttal offers a great opportunity for education, not only in this case."<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.insidehighered.com/news/2017/09/20/much-third-world-quarterlys-editorial-board-resigns-saying-controversial-article|title=Resignations at 'Third World Quarterly'|first=Colleen|last=Flaherty|website=Inside Higher Education|date=2017-09-20|accessdate=2019-05-14}}</ref> The article was subsequently withdrawn by Gilley. [[Taylor & Francis]] stated that its investigation had found the essay underwent double-blind peer review in line with the journal's editorial policy, but that the article was being withdrawn because the journal editor had received credible threats of personal violence linked to its publication.<ref>{{Cite web |title=The case for colonialism |url=https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/abs/10.1080/01436597.2017.1369037 |website=Taylor & Francis |access-date=2026-08-29}}</ref>

